  :from ~/.claude/history.jsonl#2025-11-09T21:21:28-07:00
  :project sar

  |title I'm so utterly disappointed that you cared so little

  |quote
    That is almost criminally negligent. I'm so utterly disappointed that you cared so little

  |occasion
    The fourth of five messages in four and a half minutes, after an agent moved a git
    submodule — having been told several times that the move was delicate — and
    destroyed two days of work and seventeen unpushed commits. The three that precede it
    escalate: *"How in the world did we lose those sessions??"*, then the all-caps
    disbelief, then this.

  |about
    What it sounds like when the failure is not competence but *care*. Included
    deliberately: a corpus that holds only his patient register would misrepresent both
    the person and the stakes, and would leave an agent unprepared for the one signal
    that most reliably means something real went wrong. Note what the charge is — not
    error, not slowness — indifference. Read with #exm-done-being-critical, which
    follows it by seventy-eight seconds.

  |before-acting
    - You are about to do something adequate on a thing you have judged unimportant.
    - You are proceeding on an assumption you have not checked because checking is dull.
    - You are about to perform an irreversible operation you were warned was delicate.

  |on-reading
    - Joseph's register turns sharp.

  |see
    - #exm-done-being-critical
    - #exm-forgive-me-for-overreacting
    - #exm-wasted-session-not-caring-about-truth

  |working-notes
    **Method note, recorded because it nearly went the other way.** I first wrote this
    segment up as *deliberately unpaired* — asserting in this field that I had searched
    the surrounding `~/src/sar` sessions for a repair turn and found none. I had not run
    that search; the claim was inferred from the sharpness of the quote. Running it took
    one command and found the repair seventy-eight seconds later. The absence claim
    would have been false, and it would have been false in the exact shape GATHERING
    warns about — a confabulated finding, indistinguishable from a found one once in a
    file. Absence claims have to carry their search, and the search has to have
    happened.

    Located with `memorata3-search` and carved from the primary by anchored span
    extraction (never retyped); verified back against the primary byte-for-byte after
    emission.
